NNAAMMEE
     nngghhooookk — connect to a netgraph(4) node

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     nngghhooookk [--aaddllnnSSss] [--mm _m_s_g] _p_a_t_h [_h_o_o_k_n_a_m_e]
     nngghhooookk --ee [--nn] [--mm _m_s_g] _p_a_t_h _h_o_o_k_n_a_m_e _p_r_o_g_r_a_m [_a_r_g_s _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The nngghhooookk utility creates a ng_socket(4) socket type node and connects
     it to hook _h_o_o_k_n_a_m_e of the node found at _p_a_t_h.  If _h_o_o_k_n_a_m_e is omitted,
     “debug” is assumed.

     If the --ee option is given, the third argument is interpreted as the path
     to a program, and this program is executed with the remaining arguments
     as its arguments.  Before executing, the program Netgraph messages (spec‐
     ified by the --mm option) are sent to the node.  The program is executed
     with its standard input (unless closed by --nn) and output connected to the
     hook.

     If the --ee option is not given, all data written to standard input is sent
     to the node, and all data received from the node is relayed to standard
     output.  Messages specified with --mm are sent to the node before the loop
     is entered.  The nngghhooookk utility exits when EOF is detected on standard
     input in this case.

     The options are as follows:

     --aa      Output each packet read in human-readable decoded ASCII form in‐
             stead of raw binary.

     --dd      Increase the debugging verbosity level.

     --ee      Execute the program specified by the third argument.

     --ll      Loops all received data back to the hook in addition to writing
             it to standard output.

     --mm _m_s_g  Before executing the program (in --ee mode) send the given ASCII
             control message to the node.  This option may be given more than
             once.

     --nn      Do not attempt to read any data from standard input.  The nngghhooookk
             utility will continue reading from the node until stopped by a
             signal.

     --SS      Use file descriptor 0 for output instead of the default 1.

     --ss      Use file descriptor 1 for input instead of the default 0.

